`context/renders/materials/<syllabus>/<concept>.<lang>.md` is a **material**: the guide a
learner studies. `<concept>.<lang>.slide.md` next to it is the deck. A material is derived
from a wiki page and its lesson, but it is governed by neither lane: no frontmatter, no
kind, no context lint, no derivation gate. Its first line is the only mechanical contract:
`instructions/wiki.md` gives the claim marker and the digest, and `sdt context wiki render
--check` names the fields it needs. That check reads digests, not prose.

Read this module when you write or revise a guide or a deck, after
`instructions/lesson.md` (the worked example and the transfer you inherit) and
`instructions/wiki.md` (the claims). `instructions/course.md` owns the
sequence a material sits in, and `instructions/slides.md` owns the deck's format.

## What a material is

- **A product, not a record.** Its success is a reader's: can they use it, is it
  organised, do they want to read it. The validators that govern documents end at
  `context/learning/`; a material is outside them.
- **Bound to its claims, sentence by sentence.** Every sentence that asserts a fact carries
  `<!-- claim=<page>#<id> digest=<sha> -->`; the lesson's example and transfer carry
  `<!-- lesson=<page>#worked-example -->` and `#transfer`; a gloss carries `<!-- gloss=<term> -->`.
  A sentence that needs a fact no claim carries is left out and named in the one-line
  presupposition, never invented.
- **Prose in the material's language, claims in English.** The example here is Italian;
  keep the term the claim names in English beside its gloss at first use.
- **Two files, one act.** The guide teaches; the deck is the same material for a room —
  sentence headlines supported by visual evidence, not topic phrases and bullet lists.

## The page is organised around a decision

The unit is the decision the reader must be able to take after reading. Not the topic, not
the concept's definition, not the list of objectives.

- **Open with the task.** The first section puts the reader in a concrete situation where
  the decision is needed. An objective such as "the learner will be able to…" is abstract
  and is understood only after the instruction; a demonstration of the whole task orients
  better.
- **Demonstrate once.** Carry the lesson's worked example through to its answer, in your
  words, citing its span. The example is a step, not the whole text: an expert finds a full
  worked example redundant, so the page declares the **reader's** level and lets the example
  be skipped.
- **Close with what it presupposes.** One line of what the page assumes, not a section.
  Coverage belongs to the course, not to the page.

## The four moves

A page must accomplish four things, in this order, whether or not it gives them four
headings. They are a checklist, not a template; a short page does all four in three
paragraphs.

1. **Problem** — the task, in a situation the reader recognises.
2. **Demonstration** — the worked example, carried through.
3. **Application** — what the reader does with it: a question to answer, a decision to take,
   a measurement to make. It is always present.
4. **Integration** — where it connects: the concept before, the concept after, the course.

The **exercise** is the lesson's transfer made operational. It appears only when that
transfer is a real decision — its answer changes what the reader does. It comes after the
four moves and replaces none of them. The application can be a question; the exercise is the
transfer.

## The register

- **Second person singular, present tense, active voice.** "Tracci la metrica", not "la
  metrica viene tracciata". The conditional and the future make the reader work harder.
- **One idea per paragraph**; a sentence aimed under 25 words; a list or a table where the
  content is enumerable. The number is a target, not a gate.
- **Emphasis is scarce.** Bold the term that matters once; do not set whole sentences in
  bold, and never in capitals.
- **Every term glossed at first use**, in the material's language, in a few words that add
  no fact beyond the claim. The gloss is prose beside the term — `lead time for changes
  (quanto passa da un cambiamento alla produzione)` — and the marker `<!-- gloss=<term> -->`
  only names which term it glosses. A marker without a gloss in the sentence glosses
  nothing.

## The brief

A writer does not start from a blank page. The brief has five parts and one exclusion:

1. audience and level (from the syllabus);
2. the decision the page exists for;
3. the lesson's transfer, if it is a real decision;
4. the claims, with their digests;
5. the worked example's span (`instructions/lesson.md` defines the span).

The exclusion: what the page will **not** cover, stated in one line. If a part is missing,
ask for it before writing; a page written without the decision is a summary, not a material.
A lesson with no real transfer is a legitimate case: the brief then has no part 3, and the
page has no exercise.

## The examples and the rubric

Two reference materials ship with this module — one guide and one deck, under
`context/renders/materials/`, written from real lessons. Imitate their voice and their
shape, never their content.

The rubric is a checklist, not a gate; it is what the critic and the reader test use.

- [ ] It opens with a task, not a definition or an objective list.
- [ ] The four moves are all present.
- [ ] It serves one decision and says which.
- [ ] Every term the page uses is glossed at first use or defined by the page.
- [ ] No fact appears outside a claim; what would need one is in the one-line presupposition.
- [ ] The exercise appears only if the lesson's transfer is a real decision.
- [ ] The worked example is carried through and cites its span.
- [ ] A table or a list replaces prose wherever the content is enumerable.
- [ ] The page declares the reader's level and its prerequisite in one line.
- [ ] The deck repeats the same act: sentence headlines, visual evidence, no bullet list.

## The cycle

draft → critic → revision → blind reader. The critic runs in a **clean context** — one that
has not seen the drafting conversation — reads the page and the brief, and returns a
**defect list**: what is missing, ambiguous or assumed. It never scores. The blind reader
reads the revision alone, in a fresh context with no shared state, and answers the same
three questions — what it could not find, what was ambiguous, what it assumed. Its findings
are the acceptance evidence.

The record goes in one `context/notes/` entry per material: what was drafted, from which
lesson and claims, what the critic found, what the reader found.

## The floor

The numbers a rendered page can honour: line length 45–90 characters, line spacing 120–145%
of the point size, 10–12 pt in print and 15–25 px on the web. Text contrast 4.5:1, non-text
3:1. A chart chosen by the data relationship, a palette by the data's structure. A slide
headline is a sentence, not a topic. The HTML page is the reading copy; the PDF is the save
and print export.

## The measures

Reported in the note, never enforced:

- **undefined terms** — every term the page uses that its audience would have to look up;
- **sentence-length share** — the proportion over ~25 words, as a signal;
- **the reader's defect list** — the only measure of comprehension;
- **the typographic numbers** — measure, spacing, contrast — where the artefact is rendered.

No check may fail because of them, and the rubric above is advisory in the same way. A
material that scores badly still ships, with its defects written down; a material that scores
well and cannot be used is the failure this module exists to prevent.
